---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import WordCounter from '@/components/tools/text/WordCounter.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="word-counter">
  <WordCounter client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to count words in your text</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Paste or type your text into the box. You can also drag a <code>.txt</code>, <code>.md</code> or <code>.csv</code> file onto it, or use the Open file button.</li>
      <li>Each figure updates as you type: words, characters, sentences, paragraphs, lines, reading time and speaking time.</li>
      <li>Set the reading speed for your audience. 238 words per minute is the average for adult silent reading; use 150 for dense technical writing and 350 for skimming.</li>
      <li>Check the length panel if the text has a limit: a post, a meta description, a title tag or an SMS.</li>
      <li>Copy or download the full report to record the numbers for a client brief, a submission form or a content audit.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What counts as a word, a sentence and a paragraph</h2>
    <p>
      Word counters often disagree, so these are the exact rules of this word counter. A word is a run of
      non-space characters that contains at least one letter or digit. Hyphenated compounds such as
      “state-of-the-art” count as one word, contractions such as “don’t” count as one, and a bullet or dash on
      its own counts as none. On normal prose, the result matches common word processors.
    </p>
    <p>
      A sentence ends at a full stop, question mark, exclamation mark or ellipsis followed by whitespace or the
      end of the text, and also at a blank line, so headings and list items count. Abbreviations such as
      “Dr.” and “etc.” and initials such as “J. R. R.” do not split a sentence. A paragraph is a block of
      consecutive non-empty lines; blank lines separate paragraphs.
    </p>

    <h2>Reading time and speaking time</h2>
    <p>
      Reading time is words divided by a reading speed. The 238 words-per-minute default comes from Marc
      Brysbaert’s 2019 meta-analysis of 190 studies of adult silent reading of English prose; the same paper
      gives about 183 wpm for reading aloud. Presentations and voice-overs are slower, so speaking time here
      uses 140 wpm, a comfortable conference-talk pace close to the audiobook narrator average.
    </p>
    <p>
      Both are planning estimates. Technical material with code, tables or unfamiliar terms reads much slower
      than narrative prose, and a script read aloud with pauses can take 20% longer than the figure shown.
    </p>

    <h2>Typical word counts by format</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Format</th>
            <th>Typical length</th>
            <th>Reading time</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Tweet / X post</td><td>Up to 280 characters</td><td>Seconds</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Meta description</td><td>150–155 characters</td><td>Seconds</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Product description</td><td>150–300 words</td><td>≈ 1 minute</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Blog post</td><td>800–1,500 words</td><td>4–7 minutes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>In-depth guide</td><td>2,000–4,000 words</td><td>8–17 minutes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Conference talk (20 min)</td><td>2,600–2,900 words</td><td>20 minutes spoken</td></tr>
          <tr><td>University essay</td><td>1,500–3,000 words</td><td>6–13 minutes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Novel</td><td>70,000–110,000 words</td><td>5–8 hours</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Using keyword density</h2>
    <p>
      The keyword table ranks the most frequent terms and shows the share of each in all counted terms. Set
      the phrase length to two or three words to see the phrases a search engine can match. Single-word
      density is a crude measure: “content marketing” tells you much more than “content” and “marketing”
      separately.
    </p>
    <p>
      Density is a diagnostic, not a target. If your main phrase is between about 0.5% and 2.5%, the copy
      usually reads naturally; above 4%, readers probably notice the repetition. Also check what the table
      does not contain: if the terms you want to rank for are not in the top twenty, the page is probably
      about something else.
    </p>

    <h2>Readability scores</h2>
    <p>
      The Flesch Reading Ease score combines average sentence length and average syllables per word into a
      0–100 figure, where higher is easier. Plain English for a general audience scores 60–70; technical
      documentation often scores in the 30s. The Flesch–Kincaid grade level converts the same inputs into a US
      school grade. Both measure only sentence and word length, so they cannot tell you whether the writing
      is clear. A sudden drop in the score usually means one sentence is too long.
    </p>

    <h2>Your text stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
      The counter is JavaScript in your browser tab, so your draft, a client’s confidential brief or an
      unpublished manuscript stays on your device. After the page loads, it also works offline.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
